= Deferred graph IC with cheaper algebraic descent
*Rust integration / matched adapter control / protocol51 · 17 November 2026*

The opt-in finder reuses the existing exact Nagao unit elimination. One constant-unit equation determines Im(mu); substitution leaves five equations in five variables, degree at most four. Each root lifts uniquely and replays all original six quadrics. No parameter chart or denominator-zero case is discarded. The default six-variable graph IC entry point remains available.

#image("unit-flow.svg",width:100%)

== Why this experiment
The preceding matched Run 50 shows descent dominating the new deferred graph path. The existing unit component's six fixed fixtures are a correctness/development result; they do not establish stream performance. The new hypothesis is lower full-stream work and elapsed time while preserving exact collection, accepted decompositions, affine rows and scalar recovery. Increased stream cost, changed geometry or a failed qualification rejects this successor.

The pinned library's original polynomial root finder is private. A six-variable control uses the same public root finder and copied full-only materializer as the five-variable candidate. This isolates root-adapter effects from the coordinate reduction. The original implementation is also measured.

== Controls before the call
Worker 47 and independent qualifier 162checks pass, including exhaustive small-field substitution, fixed coordinate-set and full-row agreement, malformed pivots, invalid roots and altered audit/registration records. Scoped optimized builds, strict Clippy and the ten-arm transport simulation are required before freezing. Claims and test receipts accompany the source; these are producer assertions pending independent signed review. [pcc:unverified]

Every unit attempt records its exact public-stream residual, input six quadrics, scalar-preserving replacement, reduced-system digest, lifted roots and solver flags. Independent u128 field/polynomial code reconstructs substitution, grevlex normalization and six-equation replay. Input binding intentionally shares the baseline Weil constructor. The qualifier also retains all existing norm, relation, graph, rank/kernel, affine and elliptic checks. Collection equality removes only the independently measured rank-check timer.

